<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">People Power Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1986">1986</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as EDSA Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People Power Revolution was a peaceful popular uprising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> on 22–25 February 1986 that ended the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a>. After a disputed presidential election, military rebels and huge crowds gathered on Epifanio de los Santos Avenue (EDSA) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. Marcos fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corazon-aquino">Corazon Aquino</a> became president, restoring democratic government.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> was elected president in 1965 and re-elected in 1969, and on 23 September 1972 he declared martial law under Proclamation No. 1081. He dissolved Congress, shut down critical media and ordered the arrest of opponents, among them Senator Ninoy Aquino. His spending relied heavily on debt, and after the recession of 1981 the economy declined, contracting by 6.8% in 1983 and by 7.3% in each of 1984 and 1985. On 21 August 1983 Aquino was assassinated as he disembarked at Manila International Airport after three years in exile. The killing outraged many Filipinos and led to non-cooperation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The election of 7 February 1986 pitted Marcos against Aquino's widow, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corazon-aquino">Corazon Aquino</a>. The Commission on Elections gave Marcos 10,807,197 votes to her 9,291,761, whereas the poll watcher NAMFREL's returns from 70% of precincts had her ahead by 7,835,070 to 7,053,068. On 16 February Aquino held a rally at Luneta Park, attended by about two million people, and announced a nonviolent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a> campaign and a boycott of businesses linked to Marcos.</p><p>Officers of the Reform the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armed-forces-movement">Armed Forces Movement</a> had planned a coup, and the revolt began on 22 February around Camp Crame and Camp Aguinaldo. Crowds filled Epifanio de los Santos Avenue, or EDSA. On 24 February helicopters of the 15th Strike Wing under Colonel Antonio Sotelo, sent to attack Camp Crame, landed there instead, a turning point against Marcos. On 25 February some 20,000 people blocked the Bamban Bridge and stopped loyalist reinforcements. Marcos held a rival inauguration while preparing to flee, and he and his family were flown by helicopter to Clark Air Base and then to exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Aquino issued Proclamation No. 3, a provisional constitution, and abolished the Batasang Pambansa pending a plebiscite on a new constitution. Officers of the movement that had launched the failed coup were dissatisfied with her rise to power and attempted further coups during her term. The revolution inspired later nonviolent changes of government, including those in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> and other former Soviet Bloc countries. In 2001 a similar EDSA uprising led to the resignation of President Joseph Estrada, and the radio broadcast of the revolution was inscribed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World register in 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People Power Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Martin Luther King Jr. used Gandhian methods against racial segregation in the United States, and the 1986 <b>People Power Revolution</b> in the Philippines is among later examples.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pacifism">Pacifism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Marcos dictatorship, until the <b>EDSA Revolution</b> of 1986, opposition parties, activists and unions opened their protests with the anthem, singing it with the raised clenched fist instead of the hand on the heart.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</a></li><li><blockquote>Former Philippine president Ferdinand Marcos died in Honolulu, three years after the <b>People Power Revolution</b> drove him from power.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-28">28 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ferdinand Marcos imposed martial law from 1972, ruling through cronies until the <b>People Power Revolution</b> of 1986 brought Corazon Aquino to power.</blockquote><a href="/state/philippines">Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
